(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-4-physiology-master-exam-form-b"]={"title":"Physiology Comprehensive Master Exam — Form B","category":"Physiology","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Growth hormone's carbohydrate-sparing, fat-mobilizing metabolic profile is sometimes described using which term, reflecting its tendency to raise blood glucose?","choices":["Anti-inflammatory effects","Anabolic effects","Diabetogenic effects","Natriuretic effects"],"answer":2,"exp":"GH's metabolic actions (decreased carbohydrate utilization, increased gluconeogenesis) are described using the term 'diabetogenic effects,' reflecting the tendency to raise blood glucose. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which set of findings is associated with LOW aldosterone levels?","choices":["Hyperkalemia, increased muscle excitability early on, arrhythmias/ventricular fibrillation, cell acidosis","Hypokalemia, progressive generalized muscle weakness, and cell alkalosis with a normal heart rhythm","No measurable physiological consequence on potassium, muscle, or cardiac rhythm","Hypertension and generalized peripheral edema, with no described change in serum potassium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Low aldosterone is associated with hyperkalemia, increased muscle excitability early on, arrhythmias/ventricular fibrillation, and cell acidosis. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"GnRH is secreted in pulses at approximately what interval, driving LH/FSH release?","choices":["Once every 24 hours","Once per menstrual cycle only","Every 1–3 hours","Continuously, with no pulsatile pattern"],"answer":2,"exp":"GnRH is secreted approximately every 1–3 hours, a pulsatile pattern that drives LH release (and testosterone production via LH's action on Leydig cells). — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"In the general clearance equation C = (U × V) / P, what does 'U' represent?","choices":["Urine flow rate","Plasma concentration of the substance","Ultrafiltration coefficient","Urine concentration of the substance"],"answer":3,"exp":"In the clearance equation, U is the urine concentration of the substance being measured. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"In tubuloglomerular feedback, what happens to renin release when NaCl delivery to the macula densa decreases?","choices":["Renin release increases","Renin release decreases","Renin is converted directly to angiotensin II without release","Renin release is unaffected"],"answer":0,"exp":"Decreased NaCl delivery to the macula densa increases renin release; conversely, increased NaCl delivery suppresses renin. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"During ossification, osteoblasts secrete which set of products?","choices":["Fully formed hydroxyapatite crystals ready for deposit","Type I collagen, bone sialoproteins, and osteocalcin","Cathepsin K, protons, and matrix metalloproteinases","Parathyroid hormone and calcitonin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: osteoblasts secrete mostly type I collagen along with bone sialoproteins and osteocalcin. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which hypothalamic hormone stimulates GH secretion by somatotropes?","choices":["Prolactin-inhibiting hormone (dopamine)","Growth hormone-releasing hormone (GHRH)","Growth hormone-inhibitory hormone","Gonadotropin-releasing hormone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Growth hormone-releasing hormone (GHRH) is the hypothalamic peptide that stimulates GH secretion by somatotropes. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What percentage of circulating aldosterone is bound to corticosteroid binding globulin (CBG)?","choices":["About 60%","About 10%","About 100%","About 90%"],"answer":0,"exp":"About 60% of aldosterone is bound to corticosteroid binding globulin (CBG) and other plasma proteins (albumin, prealbumin). — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which hormone stimulates testosterone production from Leydig cells, secreted every 1–3 hours from the hypothalamus?","choices":["FSH, acting on Sertoli cells","Estrogen, a downstream product","LH, downstream of pulsatile GnRH","Inhibin, a Sertoli cell product"],"answer":2,"exp":"LH, released downstream of pulsatile GnRH (secreted every 1–3 hours), stimulates testosterone production from Leydig cells. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A substance with a filterability of 1.0 is filtered:","choices":["As freely as water itself","75% as rapidly as water","Only after active transport into the filtrate","Not at all across the glomerular membrane"],"answer":0,"exp":"A filterability of 1.0 means a substance passes across the glomerular membrane as freely as water does. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Using the lecture's shorthand summary, which hormone 'adds sodium'?","choices":["ADH hormone","ANP hormone","Aldosterone","PTH hormone"],"answer":2,"exp":"In the lecture's mnemonic summary, aldosterone 'adds sodium' (drives Na+ reabsorption). — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Collagen produced by osteoblasts requires which vitamin as a cofactor for the enzyme lysyl hydroxylase?","choices":["Vitamin D (calciferol)","Vitamin C (ascorbic acid)","Vitamin K (phylloquinone)","Vitamin A (retinol)"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: ascorbic acid is the required cofactor for lysyl hydroxylase in collagen synthesis. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 47"},{"q":"Negative feedback, described as the most common hormone control mechanism, works by:","choices":["The biological effect further stimulating more hormone release, in an escalating loop","The biological effect suppressing further hormone release from the endocrine cell","Hormones only being released once per lifetime","Hormone levels rising and falling on a fixed daily clock, independent of biological effect"],"answer":1,"exp":"In negative feedback, the biological effect produced by the hormone feeds back to suppress further release from the endocrine cell — the most common control mechanism. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Between potassium and the renin-angiotensin system (RAS), how does the lecture characterize their importance in controlling aldosterone?","choices":["Neither potassium nor RAS is important; only ACTH matters for regulation","Only potassium matters for regulation; the RAS pathway is entirely irrelevant","Both are described as the most important regulators of aldosterone","Only the RAS pathway matters for regulation; potassium levels are entirely irrelevant"],"answer":2,"exp":"Both increased potassium and increased RAS activity are described as the most important regulators that increase aldosterone secretion. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"FSH and LH accelerate growth of approximately how many primary follicles per month?","choices":["1–2","6–12","20–30","Over 100"],"answer":1,"exp":"FSH (and LH) accelerate growth of approximately 6–12 primary follicles per month. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which nerve provides the parasympathetic motor supply to the detrusor muscle and carries sensory fibers detecting bladder wall stretch?","choices":["Pudendal nerve","Hypogastric nerve","Pelvic nerve","Ilioinguinal nerve"],"answer":2,"exp":"The pelvic nerves connect with the spinal cord primarily at S2–S3, carrying sensory fibers that detect bladder wall stretch and parasympathetic motor fibers terminating on ganglion cells that innervate the detrusor. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Aldosterone increases K+ secretion in which cell type?","choices":["Podocytes","Principal cells","Intercalated cells","Proximal tubule cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Aldosterone increases both Na+ reabsorption and K+ secretion in principal cells of the late distal and collecting tubules/ducts. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"How does parathyroid hormone (PTH) alter renal handling of calcium and phosphate?","choices":["It increases phosphate reabsorption in the proximal tubule and increases calcium excretion","It decreases calcium reabsorption and decreases phosphate excretion","It increases reabsorption of both calcium and phosphate","It increases calcium reabsorption in the distal tubule and increases phosphate excretion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: PTH raises distal-tubule calcium reabsorption and inhibits proximal phosphate reabsorption, increasing phosphate excretion. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 61"},{"q":"Large increases in thyroid hormone can decrease heart strength due to:","choices":["Excessive protein catabolism","Excessive protein synthesis only, with no catabolism","Decreased cardiac output","Complete cessation of cardiac contraction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Large increases in thyroid hormone can decrease heart strength due to excessive protein catabolism, in contrast to the mild strength-increasing effect seen at slight increases in thyroid hormone. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"Cortisol's effect on liver protein handling includes promoting amino acid uptake, protein synthesis, gluconeogenesis, and:","choices":["Complete cessation of liver protein activity","Decreased plasma protein formation","Increased protein catabolism in the liver, matching other tissues","Promotion of plasma protein formation in the liver"],"answer":3,"exp":"Cortisol promotes plasma protein formation in the liver, alongside promoting amino acid uptake, protein synthesis, and gluconeogenesis there. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What happens hormonally that allows a new ovulatory cycle to begin?","choices":["Estrogen and progesterone rise further, extending suppression indefinitely","LH production permanently and completely stops, with no further cycles occurring.","The corpus luteum persists indefinitely without ever involuting or losing function","Estrogen and progesterone from the corpus luteum fall, relieving suppression of GnRH/LH/FSH"],"answer":3,"exp":"As the corpus luteum involutes, estrogen and progesterone levels fall, relieving the suppression on GnRH/LH/FSH and allowing a new cycle to begin. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"What triggers the onset of the micturition reflex?","choices":["Stretch of the ureteral wall alone, independent of the bladder","A sudden drop in systemic blood pressure sensed by arterial baroreceptors","Bladder wall tension rising above a threshold level as the bladder fills","A fixed time interval elapsing since the previous voiding event"],"answer":2,"exp":"The bladder fills progressively until wall tension rises above a threshold level, at which point the micturition reflex is triggered to empty the bladder. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Per the summary table of nephron segments, which segment shows high water and high NaCl reabsorption with the key role of 'bulk reabsorption'?","choices":["Early distal convoluted tubule","Thin descending limb segment","Thick ascending limb segment","Proximal convoluted tubule (PCT)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The PCT shows high water and high NaCl reabsorption, with 'bulk reabsorption' as its key physiologic role. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"If the nerve supply to a muscle is destroyed, what happens to that muscle?","choices":["It maintains its normal tone through intact muscle spindle reflexes","It loses its tone, becomes flaccid, and eventually wastes away","It hypertrophies in response to continuous muscle spindle firing","It increases its resting tone and becomes progressively rigid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: without stimulation the fibers lose tone, become flaccid, and the muscle eventually wastes away. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 36"},{"q":"Protein and peptide hormones, such as growth hormone, ACTH, and insulin, share which general property?","choices":["Water-soluble, stored in secretory vesicles","Lipid soluble, stored freely in the cytoplasm","Bound exclusively to thyroxine-binding globulin","Derived from cholesterol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Protein and peptide hormones (3-200 amino acids) are water-soluble and stored in secretory vesicles. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Glucagon secretion is inhibited by glucose, somatostatin, and:","choices":["Fasting (a stimulating effect)","Insulin (direct effect)","Exercise (a stimulating effect)","Amino acids (a stimulating effect)"],"answer":1,"exp":"Insulin has a direct inhibitory effect on glucagon secretion, alongside glucose and somatostatin. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Testosterone's skeletal effects include increased bone thickness and calcium deposition, but prolonged exposure eventually causes:","choices":["Fusion of the epiphyses/shaft, which can decrease final height if levels are abnormally high","Bones becoming progressively more radiolucent with no change in overall density.","Continuous linear bone growth that never plateaus, slows, or reaches any eventual final limit.","Complete and permanent cessation of all bone mineralization activity throughout the skeleton."],"answer":0,"exp":"Prolonged testosterone exposure causes fusion of the bone epiphyses/shaft; abnormally high levels can cause this fusion to occur early, resulting in a net decrease in final height. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Of the four Starling forces governing GFR, which one is described as the determinant most subject to physiological control?","choices":["Colloid osmotic pressure in Bowman's capsule (πB)","Colloid osmotic pressure of glomerular plasma proteins (πG)","Hydrostatic pressure in Bowman's capsule (PB)","Glomerular hydrostatic pressure (PG)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Glomerular hydrostatic pressure (PG) is the determinant of GFR most subject to physiological control, since PB normally just changes as a function of GFR rather than driving it. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Full RAAS activation results in vasoconstriction of the efferent arterioles, Na+ reabsorption, increased ADH, and:","choices":["Suppression of the thirst center permanently","Increased thirst","Decreased thirst","No change in thirst sensation"],"answer":1,"exp":"RAAS activation results in increased thirst, alongside efferent arteriole vasoconstriction, Na+ (and water) reabsorption, and increased ADH. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"The neuromuscular junction is best defined as which of the following?","choices":["The junction of two terminal cisternae and a T-tubule","A specialized synapse between a motoneuron and a muscle fiber","An electrical gap junction linking adjacent muscle fibers","An invagination of the sarcolemma that stores calcium"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the slides define it as a specialized synapse between a motoneuron and a muscle fiber at the motor end plate. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 6"},{"q":"Gonadotropes secrete FSH and LH. Which combined actions are these hormones responsible for?","choices":["Stimulating increased hepatic gluconeogenesis and glycogen breakdown in the liver to raise blood glucose.","Stimulating the release of glucocorticoid and androgen hormones specifically from cells of the adrenal cortex.","Stimulating follicular cells in the ovary/spermatogenesis in the testis, and stimulating sex-steroid production from the gonads","Stimulating thyroid follicular cell activity and directly stimulating milk production from the mammary glands."],"answer":2,"exp":"FSH and LH stimulate follicular cells in the ovary and spermatogenesis in the testis, and stimulate production of estrogen/progesterone from the ovary and testosterone from the testis. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What triggers hypothalamic control of cortisol release, per this lecture's list of stressors?","choices":["Trauma, infection, heat/cold, surgery, and other stressors","Only genetic factors, with no environmental triggers","Only circadian rhythm, with no acute stress response","Only dietary sodium intake, with no stress-related trigger involved"],"answer":0,"exp":"Trauma, infection, heat/cold, sympathetic activation, surgery, and restraint/disease are all listed as stressors triggering hypothalamic control of cortisol release. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which hormone, increasing starting at gestation week 5 from the anterior pituitary, directly promotes milk secretion?","choices":["Relaxin","Oxytocin","hCG hormone","Prolactin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Prolactin, from the anterior pituitary, increases starting at gestation week 5 and promotes milk secretion. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"In the myogenic mechanism of renal autoregulation, increased arterial pressure leads to:","choices":["Afferent arteriole stretch, triggering smooth muscle contraction and constriction, stabilizing GFR","No vascular response at all, allowing GFR to rise passively and unchecked","Efferent arteriole dilation only, occurring with no afferent arteriolar response","Afferent arteriole relaxation, which further increases GFR beyond normal"],"answer":0,"exp":"Increased arterial pressure stretches the afferent arteriole; this stretch activates smooth muscle contraction, constricting the arteriole and preventing excessive increases in glomerular hydrostatic pressure, which helps maintain a stable GFR. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"The early distal tubule is described as 'not very permeable to urea' and also not permeable to:","choices":["Chloride","Sodium ion","Water (H2O)","Magnesium"],"answer":2,"exp":"The early distal tubule is not permeable to water, in addition to having low permeability to urea. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"During smooth-muscle excitation-contraction coupling, calcium initiates contraction by binding to:","choices":["Calmodulin, since the troponin complex is absent","Tropomyosin, displacing it directly from actin","Calsequestrin within the sarcoplasmic reticulum","Troponin C on the thin filament"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: with troponin absent, calcium binds calmodulin, which is structurally similar. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which anterior pituitary cell type produces growth hormone (somatotropin)?","choices":["Thyrotropes","Corticotropes","Somatotropes","Lactotropes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Somatotropes produce growth hormone (GH, or somatotropin). — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"How long does cortisol typically take to produce a cellular response after binding the glucocorticoid response element?","choices":["24 hours","Several days","45–60 minutes","Immediately (seconds)"],"answer":2,"exp":"Cortisol's genomic action via the glucocorticoid response element takes about 45–60 minutes to produce a cellular response. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Colostrum, the first milk, differs from mature milk in that it:","choices":["Contains essentially no protein content at all, unlike mature breast milk","Has essentially the same content as milk but with no fat","Contains essentially no maternal antibodies at all","Is produced by the breast only after weaning begins"],"answer":1,"exp":"Colostrum has essentially the same content as regular milk but contains no fat. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"Glomerular filtration rate (GFR) is defined as:","choices":["The volume of fluid filtered from the glomerular capillaries into Bowman's capsule each minute","The total volume of blood flowing through both kidneys each minute","The total volume of urine that is ultimately excreted from the bladder each minute","The overall rate of tubular reabsorption occurring along the entire nephron"],"answer":0,"exp":"GFR is the rate at which the glomeruli filter blood plasma — effectively the volume of fluid filtered from the glomerular capillaries into Bowman's capsule each minute. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which cell type of the JGA releases renin?","choices":["Extraglomerular mesangial cells","Macula densa cells","Principal cells","Granular (JG) cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Granular (JG) cells, modified smooth muscle cells, are the ones that release renin. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Myosin light chain kinase (MLCK) can phosphorylate the regulatory light chain only when:","choices":["Adenosine triphosphate levels fall below their resting baseline","Troponin C on the thin filament releases its bound calcium","Calcium is bound to calmodulin, activating the kinase","Myosin light chain phosphatase is simultaneously active"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: MLCK is active only in the presence of calmodulin that has calcium bound. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 43"},{"q":"ADH's alternate name, reflecting its vasoactive properties, is:","choices":["Corticotropin analog","Thyroxine-releasing peptide","Somatostatin","Arginine vasopressin"],"answer":3,"exp":"ADH is also called arginine vasopressin (AVP), reflecting its vasoconstrictive/pressor properties. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which best describes cortisol's site of action, per its basic hormone profile?","choices":["The adrenal cortex itself, exclusively","Only the kidney","Only the anterior pituitary","Peripheral tissues"],"answer":3,"exp":"Cortisol acts on peripheral tissues broadly. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Estrogen's effect on the fallopian tube's ciliated epithelium helps accomplish which function?","choices":["Preventing ovum transport entirely once it has left the ovarian follicle","Guiding/moving the ovum toward the tube opening (ostium) after it enters the fallopian tube","Producing progesterone locally within the fallopian tube epithelium","Actively destroying and enzymatically breaking down any unfertilized ova present."],"answer":1,"exp":"Estrogen stimulates the ciliated epithelium of the fallopian tube's fimbriated ends, which helps move the ovum toward the tube's opening (ostium), with a described 98% success rate. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"All layers of the glomerular capillary wall together act as a barrier primarily to:","choices":["Small solutes specifically, while completely blocking the passage of water molecules themselves","Red blood cells specifically, while placing no restriction at all on plasma proteins","Plasma proteins, while still permitting rapid filtration of water and most solutes","Water molecules specifically, while blocking the passage of essentially all solutes and proteins"],"answer":2,"exp":"All layers of the glomerular capillary wall provide a barrier to the filtration of plasma proteins while still permitting rapid filtration of water and most solutes. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Angiotensin II's role in urine formation includes constricting efferent arterioles and increasing filtration fraction. What does this ultimately cause?","choices":["Only water retention, with no effect on sodium","Complete and total cessation of glomerular filtration","Na+ and H2O retention, maintaining excretion of waste products","Na+ and water loss, with waste product accumulation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Angiotensin II's actions (efferent constriction, decreased peritubular capillary hydrostatic pressure, increased peritubular colloid osmotic pressure) cause Na+ and H2O retention while still maintaining excretion of waste products. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Within the sarcomere, titin is best described as which of the following?","choices":["The thick filament composed of myosin","A large filamentous structural protein","The thin filament composed of actin","The protein that caps actin at the M line"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the slides identify titin as a filamentous structural protein of the sarcomere. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 20"},{"q":"At high T3/T4 levels, protein metabolism shows which combination of effects?","choices":["Increased synthesis with no change in proteolysis","No measurable effect on either protein synthesis or protein breakdown","Decreased synthesis and decreased proteolysis","Increased synthesis and increased proteolysis (muscle wasting)"],"answer":3,"exp":"High T3/T4 increases both protein synthesis AND proteolysis, with the increased proteolysis contributing to muscle wasting. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Cortisol's carbohydrate metabolism effects include increased gluconeogenesis and:","choices":["No change at all in fasting blood glucose","Increased blood glucose (the best-known metabolic effect)","Complete cessation of hepatic glucose production","Decreased blood glucose (a hypoglycemic effect)"],"answer":1,"exp":"Cortisol increases blood glucose — described as its best-known metabolic effect — via increased gluconeogenesis and moderately decreased glucose utilization. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which hormone, secreted from granulosa/corpus luteum cells, suppresses GnRH release as part of ovarian feedback?","choices":["Inhibin","Oxytocin","ADH","Prolactin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Inhibin suppresses GnRH release as part of the ovarian feedback system. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Once the micturition reflex becomes powerful enough, it triggers a second reflex that travels through which nerve to inhibit the external sphincter?","choices":["Pelvic nerve","Hypogastric nerve","Genitofemoral nerve","Pudendal nerve"],"answer":3,"exp":"A sufficiently powerful micturition reflex triggers a second reflex that passes through the pudendal nerves to inhibit the external sphincter. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Besides hormones, which factor listed in this lecture also regulates tubular reabsorption via arterial pressure?","choices":["Only hormonal signals, with no pressure-based mechanism","Glomerulotubular balance and pressure natriuresis","Circadian rhythm alone","Body temperature regulation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Glomerulotubular balance, peritubular physical forces, the sympathetic nervous system, arterial pressure (via pressure natriuresis), and osmotic factors are all listed alongside hormones as regulators of tubular reabsorption. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which set of features correctly characterizes slow (Type I) skeletal muscle fibers?","choices":["Glycolytic, large diameter, low myoglobin content","Glycolytic, small diameter, few mitochondria","Oxidative, small diameter, high myoglobin content","Oxidative, large diameter, low capillary density"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: slow (Type I) fibers are oxidative, small in diameter, and high in myoglobin. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 32"},{"q":"Oxytocin's two described physiological functions are milk let-down and:","choices":["Uterine contraction","Water reabsorption in the kidney","Thyroid hormone synthesis","Bone growth"],"answer":0,"exp":"Oxytocin's two described functions are milk let-down and uterine contraction. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"The major actions of parathyroid hormone (PTH) include increasing plasma calcium and increasing renal production of which active compound?","choices":["1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol (active vitamin D)","Aldosterone, a mineralocorticoid hormone","Angiotensin II, a vasoconstrictor peptide","Calcitonin, PTH's functional antagonist"],"answer":0,"exp":"PTH increases renal production of 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol (active vitamin D/calcitriol), alongside directly increasing plasma calcium and promoting calcium/phosphate release from bone. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"Increased metabolic rate from testosterone contributes to which hematologic change?","choices":["Decreased hematocrit due to more red blood cells","Decreased white blood cell count","No effect on hematocrit","Increased hematocrit due to more red blood cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Testosterone's increased metabolic rate increases hematocrit, due to an increase in red blood cells. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Tubuloglomerular feedback is mediated by which cells monitoring NaCl delivery to the distal tubule?","choices":["Principal cells of the collecting duct","Intercalated cells of the collecting duct","Podocytes of the glomerular filtration barrier","Macula densa cells of the juxtaglomerular apparatus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Macula densa cells, part of the juxtaglomerular apparatus, monitor NaCl delivery in the distal tubule and mediate tubuloglomerular feedback. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"In the countercurrent multiplier's 'Single Effect' step, what happens in the thin/thick ascending limb?","choices":["Sodium is actively transported into the interstitium, concentrating it","Urea is actively secreted into the tubular lumen at this site","Water is passively reabsorbed into the surrounding interstitium","The segment suddenly becomes highly permeable to water molecules"],"answer":0,"exp":"The 'Single Effect' is active transport of sodium out of the ascending limb into the interstitium, concentrating the interstitial fluid. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Osteoclasts degrade bone matrix by releasing protons together with which enzymes across the ruffled border?","choices":["Myosin light chain kinase and calmodulin","Lysyl hydroxylase and osteocalcin","Alkaline phosphatase and osteonectin","Cathepsin K and matrix metalloproteinases"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: osteoclasts release protons plus cathepsin K and matrix metalloproteinases to degrade the matrix. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 57"}]};
